 "\nIf you want, next I can do either of these:\n\n1. ",
 StyleBox["upgrade it into a much more formal v2",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " with variational derivations, operator-theoretic definitions, and explicit \
latent-space functors, or  \n2. ",
 StyleBox["add executable training sections",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " so the notebook actually generates field data and trains a neural net on \
stability/topology classes."
}], "ChatOutput",
 TaggingRules-><|
  "CellToStringData" -> 
   "/create_nb\n# Geometric Intelligence, Multiscale Field Dynamics, RHUFT, \
and Neural Networking\n\nThis notebook is a **rigorous synthesis scaffold** \
connecting geometric intelligence, multiscale field dynamics, sensory fields, \
and neural-network-compatible representations.\n\nIt is designed to be:\n- \
**modular**\n- **symbolic-first where possible**\n- **numerically testable \
where necessary**\n- **explicit about assumptions, hypotheses, and \
speculative interpretations**\n- **compatible with neural-network pipelines \
in the Wolfram Language**\n\nIt does **not** assume metaphysical conclusions \
are already established. Instead, it provides a mathematical and \
computational framework for testing whether a multiscale field representation \
can be transformed into a resolution-stable latent representation usable by \
neural networks, and whether neural networks can be embedded back into field \
dynamics.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Epistemic policy\n\nThis notebook uses four labels \
throughout:\n\n- **Established**: standard mathematics, computation, or \
neural-network formalism.\n- **Model hypothesis**: a proposed structure that \
can be tested.\n- **Analogy**: structural similarity without identity proof.\n\
- **Speculation**: interpretation not derivable from current mathematics \
alone.\n\nThe purpose is to preserve rigor while still allowing ambitious \
synthesis.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Conceptual objective\n\nWe want a formal bridge \
between:\n\n1. **Geometry and substrate organization**\n2. **Multiscale field \
dynamics**\n3. **Resonance, persistence, and field topology**\n4. \
**Cross-scale encoding into finite resolutions**\n5. **Neural network \
consumption of field-derived features**\n6. **Bidirectional coupling between \
field and network**\n7. **RHUFT-style resolution-hierarchy transfer**\n8. \
**Mind-field interpretation as a model layer, not a prior proof**\n\nThe core \
idea is:\n\n> A sensory field may be modeled as a distributed multiscale \
state whose stable, geometry-aware, resolution-compatible summaries can serve \
as neural representations; conversely, neural systems may be understood as \
adaptive operators acting on field-derived latent structure.\n\n---\n\n## 2. \
Terminology normalization\n\n| Informal phrase | Rigorous replacement |\n\
|---|---|\n| sensory field | distributed state field with \
interaction-dependent observables |\n| field of mind | recurrent \
high-dimensional state field with feedback-selected modes |\n| intelligence \
in geometry | geometry-constrained inference / topology-aware representation \
|\n| hyperdimensional information | high-dimensional state space / modal \
coefficient space / latent manifold |\n| resonance pocket | metastable basin \
/ mode-locking region / equilibrium pocket |\n| vortex | circulation \
structure / phase winding / nontrivial curl configuration |\n| RHUFT | \
resolution-hierarchy transfer map |\n| self-measurement | recurrent internal \
selection / state-dependent feedback |\n| seamless field-network integration \
| bidirectional field-latent operator coupling |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Global setup\
\n\n```wl\nClearAll[\"Global`*\"];\n\n$Assumptions = \n  Element[{x, y, z, t, \
r, \[Theta], \[Phi], u, v, s, k, q, \[Omega], \[Gamma], \[Alpha], \[Beta], \
\[CurlyEpsilon], \[CapitalOmega], \[Lambda], \[Mu]}, Reals] && \n   \[Gamma] \
>= 0 && \[Alpha] >= 0 && \[Beta] >= 0 && \[CurlyEpsilon] > 0;\n\ncoords1 = \
{x};\ncoords2 = {x, y};\ncoords3 = {x, y, z};\ncoords4 = {t, x, y, z};\n```\n\
\n---\n\n## 4. Geometric intelligence layer\n\n### 4.1 Geometric substrate \
models\n\n**Established**: geometry constrains admissible dynamics and \
observables.\n\n```wl\nClearAll[sphereParam, torusEmbedding, radialDistance, \
geometricMetricWeight];\n\nsphereParam[\[Rho]_][\[Theta]_, \[Phi]_] := {\n  \
\[Rho] Sin[\[Theta]] Cos[\[Phi]],\n  \[Rho] Sin[\[Theta]] Sin[\[Phi]],\n  \
\[Rho] Cos[\[Theta]]\n};\n\ntorusEmbedding[majorRadius_?NumericQ, \
minorRadius_?NumericQ][u_, v_] := {\n  (majorRadius + minorRadius Cos[v]) \
Cos[u],\n  (majorRadius + minorRadius Cos[v]) Sin[u],\n  minorRadius Sin[v]\n\
};\n\nradialDistance[{x0_, y0_}][x_, y_] := Sqrt[(x - x0)^2 + (y - y0)^2];\n\n\
geometricMetricWeight[metricTensor_?MatrixQ] := \
Simplify[Sqrt[Det[metricTensor]]];\n```\n\n### 4.2 Interpretation\n\nGeometry \
is treated as an **inductive bias** on intelligence.\n\n- Spherical domains \
support closed isotropic mode families.\n- Toroidal domains support \
recirculation and doubly periodic organization.\n- Graph or lattice domains \
support discrete coupling and message propagation.\n\n**Model hypothesis**: \
geometric intelligence means that representation quality depends not only on \
data values, but on domain topology and invariant structure.\n\n---\n\n## 5. \
Multiscale field dynamics layer\n\n### 5.1 General field equation\n\nWe study \
systems of the form:\n\n$$\\partial_t^2 u + 2\\gamma\\,\\partial_t u + \
\\mathcal{L}u + \\mathcal{N}(u) = f$$\n\nwhere:\n- $$u$$ is the field state,\n\
- $$\\mathcal{L}$$ is a geometry-dependent linear operator,\n- \
$$\\mathcal{N}(u)$$ is nonlinear coupling,\n- $$f$$ is forcing,\n- \
$$\\gamma$$ is damping.\n\nThis equation is not yet a final ontology. It is a \
broad test class.\n\n### 5.2 Matrix surrogate tools\n\n```wl\nClearAll[\n  \
dampedLaplacian1D,\n  spectralData,\n  truncationStabilityTable,\n  qFactor,\n\
  modeEnergy\n];\n\ndampedLaplacian1D[n_Integer?Positive, \[Gamma]_?NumericQ] \
:= Module[\n  {lap},\n  lap = SparseArray[\n      {\n        Band[{1, 1}] -> \
-2,\n        Band[{1, 2}] -> 1,\n        Band[{2, 1}] -> 1\n      },\n      \
{n, n}\n    ] // Normal;\n  lap - \[Gamma] IdentityMatrix[n]\n];\n\n\
spectralData[matrix_?MatrixQ] := Module[\n  {vals},\n  vals = Eigenvalues[N @ \
matrix];\n  <|\n    \"Eigenvalues\" -> vals,\n    \"RealParts\" -> Re[vals],\n\
    \"ImaginaryParts\" -> Im[vals],\n    \"ContinuousTimeStableQ\" -> And @@ \
Thread[Re[vals] < 0],\n    \"DiscreteTimeStableQ\" -> And @@ Thread[Abs[vals] \
< 1],\n    \"StabilityMarginContinuous\" -> -Max[Re[vals]],\n    \
\"StabilityMarginDiscrete\" -> 1 - Max[Abs[vals]]\n  |>\n];\n\n\
truncationStabilityTable[operatorBuilder_, modeCounts_List] := \n  \
AssociationMap[spectralData[operatorBuilder[#]] &, modeCounts];\n\nqFactor[\
\[Omega]_?NumericQ, \[Gamma]_?NumericQ] /; \[Gamma] > 0 := \[Omega]/(2 \
\[Gamma]);\nqFactor[\[Omega]_?NumericQ, 0] := Infinity;\n\nmodeEnergy[a_, \
adot_, \[Omega]_?NumericQ] := 1/2 (adot^2 + \[Omega]^2 a^2);\n```\n\n### 5.3 \
Spectral convergence diagnostics\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[spectralConvergenceReport];\n\n\
spectralConvergenceReport[operatorBuilder_, modeCounts_List] := Module[\n  \
{table, margins},\n  table = truncationStabilityTable[operatorBuilder, \
modeCounts];\n  margins = AssociationMap[table[#][\"StabilityMarginContinuous\
\"] &, modeCounts];\n  <|\n    \"Table\" -> table,\n    \"Margins\" -> \
margins,\n    \"ConvergentMarginQ\" -> \
Quiet[Length[DeleteDuplicates[Round[Values[margins], 10^-6]]] <= 3]\n  |>\n];\
\n```\n\n**Rigorous requirement**: a persistence claim should not depend \
arbitrarily on truncation.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Continuous eigenmode layer\n\n### \
6.1 One-dimensional bounded domain\n\n```wl\nClearAll[oneDPDEEigensystem];\n\n\
oneDPDEEigensystem[n_Integer?Positive] := \n  NDEigensystem[\n    {\n      \
-u''[x],\n      DirichletCondition[u[x] == 0, x == 0 || x == 1]\n    },\n    \
u[x],\n    {x, 0, 1},\n    n\n  ];\n```\n\n### 6.2 Two-dimensional circular \
cavity\n\n```wl\nClearAll[circularRegion, circularModes];\n\ncircularRegion = \
Disk[];\n\ncircularModes[n_Integer?Positive] := \n  NDEigensystem[\n    {\n   \
   -Laplacian[u[x, y], {x, y}],\n      DirichletCondition[u[x, y] == 0, True]\
\n    },\n    u[x, y],\n    {x, y} \[Element] circularRegion,\n    n\n  ];\n\
```\n\n### 6.3 Interpretation\n\nA resonant cavity is made rigorous by:\n\n1. \
geometry,\n2. operator,\n3. boundary conditions,\n4. eigenmodes,\n5. damping \
and forcing.\n\n**Model hypothesis**: sensory fields inherit representational \
structure from admissible eigenmodes.\n\n---\n\n## 7. RHUFT: \
Resolution-Hierarchy Transfer\n\n### 7.1 Definition\n\nRHUFT is introduced \
here as a **resolution-hierarchy transfer map**:\n\n> a family of \
transformations that carry field states between resolutions while preserving \
diagnostically relevant structure.\n\nThis is not a standard built-in term; \
it is a modeling construct.\n\n### 7.2 Dyadic transfer operators\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[\n  dyadicCoarseGrain,\n  dyadicLift,\n  rgOrbit,\n  scaleResiduals,\
\n  fixedScaleResidual,\n  scaleFixedPointData\n];\n\n\
dyadicCoarseGrain[list_List] /; EvenQ[Length[list]] := Mean /@ \
Partition[list, 2];\n\ndyadicLift[list_List] := Riffle[list, list];\n\n\
rgOrbit[transform_, state_List, steps_Integer?NonNegative] := \n  \
NestWhileList[\n    transform,\n    N @ state,\n    EvenQ[Length[#]] && \
Length[#] > 1 &,\n    1,\n    steps\n  ];\n\nscaleResiduals[state_List, \
steps_Integer:8] := Module[\n  {orbit, normalizedOrbit},\n  orbit = \
rgOrbit[dyadicCoarseGrain, state, steps];\n  normalizedOrbit = Normalize[#, \
Norm] & /@ orbit;\n  Table[\n    Norm[\n      \
Normalize[dyadicCoarseGrain[normalizedOrbit[[i]]], Norm] - normalizedOrbit[[i \
+ 1]],\n      2\n    ],\n    {i, 1, Length[normalizedOrbit] - 1}\n  ]\n];\n\n\
fixedScaleResidual[state_List] /; EvenQ[Length[state]] := Module[\n  {coarse, \
lifted},\n  coarse = dyadicCoarseGrain[state];\n  lifted = \
Take[dyadicLift[coarse], Length[state]];\n  Norm[Normalize[lifted, Norm] - \
Normalize[state, Norm], 2]\n];\n\nscaleFixedPointData[state_List, \
steps_Integer:8, tol_:10^-6] := <|\n  \"Residuals\" -> scaleResiduals[state, \
steps],\n  \"FixedScaleResidual\" -> fixedScaleResidual[state],\n  \
\"FixedPointQ\" -> fixedScaleResidual[state] < tol\n|>;\n```\n\n### 7.3 \
Interpretation\n\nA field representation is resolution-usable if it survives \
transfer across scale with bounded distortion.\n\nThis is the central bridge \
to neural networking:\n\n> the network should not depend on a representation \
that collapses under mild resolution change.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Sensory field \
formalization\n\n### 8.1 Working definition\n\nA **sensory field** is modeled \
here as a distributed state field with:\n\n- local source structure,\n- \
geometry-conditioned propagation,\n- multiscale observability,\n- \
repeated-interaction selection,\n- field-topological organization.\n\n### 8.2 \
Source-induced field potential\n\n```wl\nClearAll[sourceFieldPotential, \
effectivePotential2D, effectiveForce2D, curl2D];\n\n\
sourceFieldPotential[sources_List][x_, y_] := Total[\n  Map[\n    \
Function[{src},\n      With[{center = src[[1]], amp = src[[2]], width = \
src[[3]]},\n        amp Exp[-((x - center[[1]])^2 + (y - center[[2]])^2)/(2 \
width^2)]\n      ]\n    ],\n    sources\n  ]\n];\n\n\
effectivePotential2D[pot_, \[CapitalOmega]_?NumericQ][x_, y_] := pot[x, y] - \
1/2 \[CapitalOmega]^2 (x^2 + y^2);\n\neffectiveForce2D[pot_, \
\[CapitalOmega]_?NumericQ][x_, y_] := \
Evaluate[-Grad[effectivePotential2D[pot, \[CapitalOmega]][x, y], {x, y}]];\n\n\
curl2D[field_][x_, y_] := D[field[[2]], x] - D[field[[1]], y];\n```\n\n### \
8.3 Interpretation\n\nThe sensory field is not treated as raw metaphysics. It \
is a testable structure in which:\n\n- sources define local density \
contrasts,\n- effective potentials define pockets,\n- gradients define force \
or attention flow,\n- curl or winding defines circulation structure.\n\n\
**Analogy**: these may play the role of salience channels, preconceptual \
attractors, or recurrent perceptual organization.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Selection \
stability and recurrent realization\n\n### 9.1 Repeated interaction channel\n\
\n```wl\nClearAll[dephaseStep, quasiPeriodicSchedule, pointerModeData];\n\n\
dephaseStep[state_?MatrixQ, phase_, p_?NumericQ] := Module[\n  {unitary, \
mixed},\n  unitary = DiagonalMatrix[Exp[I phase Range[Length[state]]]];\n  \
mixed = unitary.state.ConjugateTranspose[unitary];\n  (1 - p) mixed + p \
DiagonalMatrix[Diagonal[mixed]]\n];\n\n\
quasiPeriodicSchedule[steps_Integer?Positive, dim_Integer?Positive] := \n  \
Table[Table[N[2 Pi n/(j + 1)], {j, 1, dim}], {n, 1, steps}];\n\n\
pointerModeData[initialState_?MatrixQ, phaseSchedule_List, p_?NumericQ] := \
Module[\n  {orbit},\n  orbit = FoldList[dephaseStep[#1, #2, p] &, \
initialState, phaseSchedule];\n  <|\n    \"Orbit\" -> orbit,\n    \
\"CoherenceNorms\" -> (Norm[# - DiagonalMatrix[Diagonal[#]], \"Frobenius\"] & \
/@ orbit),\n    \"PointerWeights\" -> Diagonal[Last[orbit]]\n  |>\n];\n```\n\n\
### 9.2 Interpretation\n\nA state becomes operationally real in this \
framework if it persists under repeated interaction channels.\n\nThis is a \
formal replacement for loose phrases like \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]measured \
into existence.\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]\n\n---\n\n## 10. Neural-network-ready \
field encoding\n\n### 10.1 Objective\n\nWe need a map from sensory-field \
structure to a finite-dimensional representation usable by a neural net.\n\n\
Let the encoder be:\n\n$$E: \\mathcal{F} \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$$\n\nwhere \
$$\\mathcal{F}$$ is a field state space and $$\\mathbb{R}^n$$ is a latent \
vector or tensor space.\n\n### 10.2 Field feature extractor\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[fieldFeatureVector];\n\nfieldFeatureVector[state_List] := <|\n  \
\"Length\" -> Length[state],\n  \"Norm2\" -> Norm[state, 2],\n  \"Norm1\" -> \
Norm[state, 1],\n  \"Mean\" -> Mean[state],\n  \"Variance\" -> \
Variance[state],\n  \"FixedScaleResidual\" -> fixedScaleResidual[state],\n  \
\"ScaleResidualMean\" -> Mean[scaleResiduals[state, Min[8, \
Log2[Length[state]] - 1]]]\n|>;\n```\n\n### 10.3 Tensor encoder for arrays\n\n\
```wl\nClearAll[fieldArrayEncoder];\n\nfieldArrayEncoder[array_?ArrayQ] := N \
@ Flatten[array];\n```\n\n### 10.4 Interpretation\n\nThis is the first \
rigorous bridge:\n\n- sensory field dynamics generate structured states,\n- \
RHUFT tests whether they are stable across resolution,\n- stable states are \
encoded into vectors/tensors,\n- those vectors become neural network inputs.\n\
\nSo a sensory field becomes \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]resolution usable\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] when its latent summary remains stable enough for \
learned processing.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Neural network layer\n\n### 11.1 Minimal \
field-to-network model\n\n**Established**: symbolic neural nets can consume \
numeric vector or tensor encodings.\n\n```wl\nClearAll[minimalFieldNet];\n\n\
minimalFieldNet[inputDim_Integer?Positive, outputDim_Integer?Positive] := \
NetChain[{\n   LinearLayer[32],\n   Ramp,\n   LinearLayer[32],\n   Ramp,\n   \
LinearLayer[outputDim]\n  }, \"Input\" -> inputDim];\n```\n\n### 11.2 \
Geometry-aware variant\n\n```wl\nClearAll[geometryAwareFieldNet];\n\n\
geometryAwareFieldNet[inputDim_Integer?Positive, outputDim_Integer?Positive] \
:= NetGraph[\n  <|\n    \"lin1\" -> LinearLayer[64],\n    \"act1\" -> Ramp,\n \
   \"lin2\" -> LinearLayer[32],\n    \"act2\" -> Ramp,\n    \"out\" -> \
LinearLayer[outputDim]\n  |>,\n  {\n    NetPort[\"Input\"] -> \"lin1\" -> \
\"act1\" -> \"lin2\" -> \"act2\" -> \"out\"\n  },\n  \"Input\" -> inputDim\n\
];\n```\n\n### 11.3 Interpretation\n\nA neural network here is not treated as \
separate from the field. It is treated as an adaptive operator on \
field-derived coordinates.\n\n**Model hypothesis**: intelligence may arise \
when field structure and learned operators become mutually compatible \
descriptions of the same organization.\n\nSee also \
[NetChain](paclet:ref/NetChain), [NetGraph](paclet:ref/NetGraph), \
[NetTrain](paclet:ref/NetTrain), \
[NetMeasurements](paclet:ref/NetMeasurements), and \
[NetModel](paclet:ref/NetModel).\n\n---\n\n## 12. Bidirectional field-network \
coupling\n\n### 12.1 Encoder-decoder abstraction\n\nWe define three maps:\n\n\
- field encoder $$E: \\mathcal{F} \\to \\mathcal{Z}$$\n- network dynamics \
$$N: \\mathcal{Z} \\to \\mathcal{Z}$$\n- field decoder $$D: \\mathcal{Z} \\to \
\\mathcal{F}$$\n\nThe coupled loop is:\n\n$$\\mathcal{F} \\xrightarrow{E} \
\\mathcal{Z} \\xrightarrow{N} \\mathcal{Z} \\xrightarrow{D} \\mathcal{F}$$\n\n\
### 12.2 Simple symbolic prototype\n\n```wl\nClearAll[fieldEncoder, \
latentOperator, fieldDecoder, coupledFieldNetworkStep];\n\n\
fieldEncoder[state_List] := fieldArrayEncoder[state];\n\n\
latentOperator[vec_List] := Normalize[vec + 0.01 RotateLeft[vec], Norm];\n\n\
fieldDecoder[vec_List, len_Integer?Positive] := Take[vec, len];\n\n\
coupledFieldNetworkStep[state_List] := Module[\n  {latent},\n  latent = \
latentOperator[fieldEncoder[state]];\n  fieldDecoder[latent, Length[state]]\n\
];\n```\n\n### 12.3 Interpretation\n\nThis is a placeholder architecture \
expressing the idea that field and network can form a single iterative \
system.\n\n**Speculation**: in a sufficiently rich theory, the distinction \
between \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]field\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] and \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]network\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] may become \
representation-dependent rather than ontologically fundamental.\n\n---\n\n## \
13. Mind-field layer\n\n### 13.1 Careful statement\n\nThe claim that \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]the mind also has a field\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] is \
not established by the mathematics here.\n\nHowever, the notebook provides a \
disciplined way to model a **mind-field hypothesis**:\n\n> a recurrent, \
high-dimensional, geometry-sensitive, interaction-selected state field \
supporting stable modes, circulation structure, and latent encodings usable \
for adaptive inference.\n\n### 13.2 Minimal formal interpretation\n\nA \
mind-field candidate should exhibit all of the following:\n\n1. persistent \
modes,\n2. bounded scale distortion,\n3. selective stabilization under \
recurrent coupling,\n4. structured attractor or circulation topology,\n5. \
interpretable latent embedding for downstream computation.\n\nThis is still a \
model hypothesis, not a proof of consciousness ontology.\n\n---\n\n## 14. \
Synthesis report\n\n### 14.1 Combined diagnostics\n\n```wl\n\
ClearAll[multiscaleResonanceReport, synthesisEvidenceScore];\n\n\
multiscaleResonanceReport[matrix_?MatrixQ, state_List, \
initialDensity_?MatrixQ, phaseSchedule_List, p_?NumericQ, steps_Integer:6, \
tol_:10^-6] := Module[\n  {spec, scale, selection, verdict},\n  spec = \
spectralData[matrix];\n  scale = scaleFixedPointData[state, steps, tol];\n  \
selection = pointerModeData[initialDensity, phaseSchedule, p];\n  verdict = \
<|\n    \"SpectrallyStableQ\" -> TrueQ[spec[\"ContinuousTimeStableQ\"]],\n    \
\"ScaleRobustQ\" -> TrueQ[scale[\"FixedPointQ\"]],\n    \"SelectionStableQ\" \
-> Max[selection[\"PointerWeights\"]] > 1/2\n  |>;\n  <|\n    \"Spectral\" -> \
spec,\n    \"Scale\" -> scale,\n    \"Selection\" -> selection,\n    \
\"Verdict\" -> verdict,\n    \"AllThreeQ\" -> And @@ Values[verdict],\n    \
\"FixedScaleResidual\" -> fixedScaleResidual[state]\n  |>\n];\n\n\
synthesisEvidenceScore[report_Association] := Mean @ Boole @ \
Values[report[\"Verdict\"]];\n```\n\n### 14.2 Interpretation\n\nA \
field-derived representation is strongest when it is:\n\n- spectrally \
persistent,\n- scale robust,\n- interaction-selected.\n\nThis avoids \
collapsing all claims into a single vague notion of \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]emergence.\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]\n\n---\n\n## 15. \
Visual diagnostics\n\n```wl\nClearAll[\n  spectralPanel,\n  scalePanel,\n  \
pointerPanel,\n  effectivePotentialPlot,\n  fieldCirculationPlot\n];\n\n\
spectralPanel[matrix_?MatrixQ] := Module[\n  {vals = Eigenvalues[N @ \
matrix]},\n  GraphicsGrid[{{\n     ListPlot[\n       Transpose[{Re[vals], \
Im[vals]}],\n       Frame -> True,\n       AspectRatio -> 1,\n       \
PlotLabel -> \"Eigenvalues in complex plane\",\n       FrameLabel -> {\"Re\", \
\"Im\"},\n       Epilog -> {Red, Dashed, Line[{{0, -10}, {0, 10}}]}\n     ],\n\
     BarChart[Re[vals], Frame -> True, PlotLabel -> \"Real parts of \
eigenvalues\"]\n   }}]\n];\n\nscalePanel[state_List, steps_Integer:6] := \
Module[\n  {orbit, residuals},\n  orbit = rgOrbit[dyadicCoarseGrain, state, \
steps];\n  residuals = scaleResiduals[state, steps];\n  GraphicsGrid[{{\n     \
ListLinePlot[state, Frame -> True, PlotLabel -> \"Initial state\"],\n     \
ListLinePlot[residuals, Frame -> True, PlotLabel -> \"Scale residuals\"]\n   \
}}]\n];\n\npointerPanel[initialState_?MatrixQ, phaseSchedule_List, \
p_?NumericQ] := Module[\n  {data, pops},\n  data = \
pointerModeData[initialState, phaseSchedule, p];\n  pops = Diagonal /@ \
data[\"Orbit\"];\n  GraphicsGrid[{{\n     \
ListLinePlot[data[\"CoherenceNorms\"], Frame -> True, PlotLabel -> \
\"Off-diagonal coherence\"],\n     ListLinePlot[Transpose[pops], Frame -> \
True, PlotLabel -> \"Pointer populations\", PlotLegends -> Automatic]\n   }}]\
\n];\n\neffectivePotentialPlot[pot_, \[CapitalOmega]_?NumericQ, xrange_, \
yrange_] := \n  Plot3D[\n    Evaluate[effectivePotential2D[pot, \
\[CapitalOmega]][x, y]],\n    {x, xrange[[1]], xrange[[2]]},\n    {y, \
yrange[[1]], yrange[[2]]},\n    PlotRange -> All,\n    Mesh -> None,\n    \
PlotLabel -> \"Effective potential landscape\",\n    AxesLabel -> {\"x\", \"y\
\", \"Veff\"}\n  ];\n\nfieldCirculationPlot[field_, xrange_, yrange_] := \
Module[\n  {curlExpr},\n  curlExpr = curl2D[field][x, y];\n  GraphicsGrid[{{\n\
     VectorPlot[\n       Evaluate[field],\n       {x, xrange[[1]], \
xrange[[2]]},\n       {y, yrange[[1]], yrange[[2]]},\n       PlotLabel -> \
\"Field flow\"\n     ],\n     DensityPlot[\n       Evaluate[curlExpr],\n      \
 {x, xrange[[1]], xrange[[2]]},\n       {y, yrange[[1]], yrange[[2]]},\n      \
 PlotLabel -> \"Scalar curl\"\n     ]\n   }}]\n];\n```\n\n---\n\n## 16. \
Example workflow\n\n```wl\nexampleMatrix = dampedLaplacian1D[12, 0.4];\n\
exampleState = Normalize[Table[1./Sqrt[i], {i, 1, 64}], Norm];\n\
exampleDensity = {{1., 0.8}, {0.8, 1.}}/2;\nexamplePhases = \
quasiPeriodicSchedule[30, 2];\n\nexampleSources = {\n  {{-1.5, 0.0}, 1.0, \
1.2},\n  {{1.0, 0.8}, 0.8, 0.9},\n  {{0.5, -1.2}, 0.6, 1.1}\n};\n\n\
examplePotential = sourceFieldPotential[exampleSources];\nexampleField = \
effectiveForce2D[examplePotential, 0.35][x, y];\n\nexampleReport = \
multiscaleResonanceReport[\n  exampleMatrix,\n  exampleState,\n  \
exampleDensity,\n  examplePhases,\n  0.15,\n  5,\n  10^-6\n];\n\n\
exampleFeatures = fieldFeatureVector[exampleState];\nexampleInput = \
Values[exampleFeatures] // N;\nexampleNet = \
minimalFieldNet[Length[exampleInput], 3];\n```\n\nInterpretation:\n\n- the \
field state is tested for scale robustness,\n- the dynamics are tested for \
spectral persistence,\n- selection under recurrence is tested,\n- features \
are extracted into a finite vector,\n- the vector becomes usable as neural \
input.\n\n---\n\n## 17. Neural training notes\n\nIf you want to train neural \
models on field-derived data, use [NetTrain](paclet:ref/NetTrain). Related \
symbols include [NetModel](paclet:ref/NetModel), \
[NetMeasurements](paclet:ref/NetMeasurements), \
[NetTrainResultsObject](paclet:ref/NetTrainResultsObject), and \
[NetStateObject](paclet:ref/NetStateObject).\n\nFor example, training \
progress can include precision and recall measurements; see \
[NetTrain](paclet:ref/NetTrain#152404673).\n\nA realistic next step is:\n\n1. \
generate many field states under varied parameters,\n2. compute scale-stable \
feature vectors,\n3. label them by stability class, topology class, or pocket \
count,\n4. train a classifier or regressor on those features,\n5. test \
whether the learned model generalizes across resolution.\n\n---\n\n## 18. \
Falsification conditions\n\nA serious framework must specify failure modes.\n\
\nThis synthesis is under pressure if:\n\n1. scale-stable field summaries do \
not remain usable for learning,\n2. field-derived features are less robust \
than naive raw encodings,\n3. spectral stability fails to correlate with \
useful latent structure,\n4. apparent circulation has no quantitative curl or \
winding signature,\n5. network performance collapses under modest resolution \
changes,\n6. bidirectional field-network coupling produces instability rather \
than representational gain,\n7. the mind-field layer adds no predictive power \
beyond ordinary state-space models.\n\n---\n\n## 19. Final synthesis \
statement\n\nThis notebook provides a rigorous scaffold for investigating \
whether:\n\n- geometry constrains intelligence-relevant representations,\n- \
multiscale field dynamics generate persistent structure,\n- RHUFT-style \
transfer preserves usable information across resolution,\n- sensory fields \
can be encoded into neural-network-compatible latents,\n- neural networks can \
act as adaptive operators on field-derived structure,\n- field and network \
can form a seamless bidirectional loop.\n\nIt is a **test harness for \
geometric intelligence and multiscale field-network synthesis**, not a proof \
that all metaphysical interpretations are already established.\n\n---\n\n## \
20. Suggested next extensions\n\n1. Replace dyadic coarse-graining with \
wavelet or graph-coarsening transforms.\n2. Add PDE-constrained encoders \
using eigencoefficient projections.\n3. Construct field-image datasets and \
train convolutional or graph neural nets.\n4. Add recurrent latent dynamics \
using [NetStateObject](paclet:ref/NetStateObject).\n5. Compare direct field \
simulation against learned surrogate dynamics.\n6. Formalize topology \
features: winding number, persistent homology, Morse structure.\n7. Introduce \
causal graph or multiway branches for alternative update histories using the \
reviewed Resource Function approach for multiway systems.\n\n/exec\nRESULT\n\
